ttps://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/2705","industries":[{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"Immigration law firms would see changes in demand for services related to family-based visa petitions and the new nonimmigrant parent visa category."},{"industry":"hospitality","confidence":0.4,"rationale":"Reduced family immigration could affect demand for travel and lodging services related to family visits and relocation."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would sharply reduce the number of family-based immigration visas available each year, lowering the annual cap from 480,000 to 88,000. It would eliminate visa categories for siblings and married children of U.S. citizens, and would remove parents of citizens from the immediate-relative category that currently has no numerical limit. Parents of citizens would instead be eligible for a temporary visa that does not allow them to work or receive public benefits.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"People applying to live here, and their families here.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"K000405","score":0.03341706325741149},"overlapCount":41},"meaning":{"percentile":64.60009465215335,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.6192111669113716}},{"billId":"119-hr-27","bioguideId":"C001118","cycle":2026,"score":0.013531003096342246,"matches":[{"industry":"legal","donorAmount":23750,"donorShare":0.022592674866922964,"billConfidence":0.6,"contribution":0.009348693048381916},{"industry":"pharma","donorAmount":7500,"donorShare":0.007134528905344093,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.00418231004796033}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.6192111669113716},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-27","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"27","title":"HALT Fentanyl Act","introducedDate":"2025-01-03","latestActionDate":"2025-02-10","latestActionText":"Received in the Senate and Read twice and referred to the Committee on the Judiciary.","policyArea":"Crime and Law Enforcement","subjects":["Administrative law and regulatory procedures","Department of Justice","Drug trafficking and controlled substances","Licensing and registrations","Research administration and funding"],"sponsorBioguideId":"G000568","cosponsorCount":59,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/27","industries":[{"industry":"pharma","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill changes registration and licensing requirements for pharmaceutical researchers working with controlled substances and affects the regulatory classification of fentanyl-related compounds."},{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"The bill establishes new criminal penalties and quantity thresholds that will affect defense attorneys and prosecutors handling drug trafficking cases."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would permanently classify all fentanyl-related substances as Schedule I controlled substances, meaning they would be treated as having high abuse potential and no accepted medical use. It sets the same criminal penalties and quantity thresholds for fentanyl-related substances as currently apply to fentanyl analogues. The bill also creates a streamlined registration process for researchers studying Schedule I substances and makes it easier for registered researchers to conduct certain activities without additional permits.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"People caught up in the criminal law, and victims of crime.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"H001086","score":0.08496351694573398},"overlapCount":58},"meaning":{"percentile":58.82631329862754,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.6192111669113716}},{"billId":"119-hr-29","bioguideId":"C001118","cycle":2026,"score":0.010973584935362582,"matches":[{"industry":"legal","donorAmount":23750,"donorShare":0.022592674866922964,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.010973584935362582}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.6192111669113716},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-29","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"29","title":"Laken Riley Act","introducedDate":"2025-01-03","latestActionDate":"2025-02-10","latestActionText":"Read the second time. Placed on Senate Legislative Calendar under General Orders. Calendar No. 10.","policyArea":"Immigration","subjects":["Border security and unlawful immigration","Civil actions and liability","Crimes against property","Criminal investigation, prosecution, interrogation","Detention of persons","Immigration status and procedures","Intergovernmental relations","Refugees, asylum, displaced persons","State and local government operations","Visas and passports"],"sponsorBioguideId":"C001129","cosponsorCount":53,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/29","industries":[{"industry":"government","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"The bill directly imposes new detention mandates on DHS and creates new litigation exposure for federal agencies, affecting federal and state government operations and budgets."},{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill creates new grounds for state litigation against the federal government and expands detention requirements, increasing demand for legal services in immigration and intergovernmental litigation."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would require the Department of Homeland Security to detain individuals who are in the country without authorization and have been arrested for or charged with burglary, theft, larceny, or shoplifting. It would also allow state governments to sue the federal government for injunctive relief when federal immigration enforcement decisions or failures—such as releasing individuals from custody or not fulfilling visa or asylum processing requirements—cause harm to the state or its residents, including financial harm exceeding $100.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"People applying to live here, and their families here. People applying to come here, and their families here.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"S000250","score":0.02595346470272257},"overlapCount":50},"meaning":{"percentile":50.87553241836251,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.6192111669113716}},{"billId":"119-hr-685","bioguideId":"C001118","cycle":2026,"score":0.009380584301470938,"matches":[{"industry":"health-providers","donorAmount":12500,"donorShare":0.011890881508906822,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.005615138490317111},{"industry":"pharma","donorAmount":7500,"donorShare":0.007134528905344093,"billConfidence":0.95,"contribution":0.003765445811153827}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.6192111669113716},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-685","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"685","title":"SAVE Moms and Babies Act of 2025","introducedDate":"2025-01-23","latestActionDate":"2025-01-23","latestActionText":"Referred to the House Committee on Energy and Commerce.","policyArea":"Health","subjects":["Abortion","Drug safety, medical device, and laboratory regulation","Health information and medical records","Health personnel","Health technology, devices, supplies","Marketing and advertising","Prescription drugs"],"sponsorBioguideId":"L000566","cosponsorCount":62,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/685","industries":[{"industry":"pharma","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill directly prohibits approval of new abortion drugs and imposes significant new regulatory restrictions on already-approved drugs in this category."},{"industry":"health-providers","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill creates new certification requirements for prescribing practitioners and mandates they have surgical intervention capability, changing practice obligations."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would prohibit the FDA from approving any new drugs intended to terminate pregnancy. For drugs already approved for this purpose, it would impose new restrictions: requiring in-person dispensing by a specially certified practitioner, prohibiting use after 70 days of gestation, and mandating that the practitioner be able to provide surgical intervention. The bill would also prevent practitioners from both prescribing and dispensing these medications.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who fills a prescription. Anyone who takes a medicine.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"H001086","score":0.11251216543663692},"overlapCount":62},"meaning":{"percentile":44.675816374822524,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.6192111669113716}},{"billId":"119-hjres-20","bioguideId":"C001118","cycle":2026,"score":0.007796687797212632,"matches":[{"industry":"energy-fossil","donorAmount":13000,"donorShare":0.012366516769263095,"billConfidence":0.7,"contribution":0.003394730093523203},{"industry":"utilities-electric","donorAmount":11000,"donorShare":0.010463975727838004,"billConfidence":0.5,"contribution":0.002051759946634903},{"industry":"real-estate","donorAmount":13375,"donorShare"